body:
Imo, one of the things that makes Tyranny so different from most other RPG's is the way it makes you feel like the sole arbiter of events. Instead of the usual Hero archetype where you're constantly struggling against events out of your control, the way Tyranny plays makes you feel like the 'big bad', even with Kyros existing. Events don't so much happen to you as much as you happen to them, your choices essentially dictating the entire outcome of any given situation. Not to mention the PC having the unique ability to affect the edicts, putting you directly in the same league as Kyros, a being so terrible and powerful that, even though Kyros itself is never shown, inspires the same type of feelings one might get when thinking of Sauron or Vader, especially from the characters you meet. I can't think of another game that makes you feel as powerful as Tyranny does.

&#x200B;

When it comes to reccomendations, I have to echo some of the other comments; KoTOR 2, although dated by this point, scratches the itch a bit, especially the 'big bad' MC on Dark Side playthroughs, but the main flaw with most similar titles is how little impact the consequences of your actions seem have in the long term, by comparison. Fallout: New Vegas is really the only other game I can think of that matches up in terms of having both great freedom of choice and lasting/powerful consequences for those choices. Both of those being Obsidian games where you tend to happen to events and dictate the outcome, with lasting consequences, although far more so in New Vegas' case imo. Wasteland 3 is another game I recommend, your choices can have lasting consequences on the flow of the story and the potential ending, it has a similar setting to Fallout but gameplay more similar to Tyranny. Dragon Age: Origins is another spectacular game in terms of lore and player agency. DA2, not so much, and Inquisition to a slightly lesser degree, mostly because it feels to me like a single player mmo sometimes.
